 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the state finance law, in relation to providing for\ndiscretionary buying thresholds",
    "basePrintNo": "S6998",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6998-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§163 & 112, St Fin L",
    "lawSection": "State Finance Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6998\n\nSPONSOR: REICHLIN-MELNICK\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the state finance law, in relation to providing for\ndiscretionary buying thresholds\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThe discretionary purchasing limit for the OGS purchase of services and\ncommodities is $85,000. This bill would increase the discretionary\npurchase limit for services and commodities for transactions processed\nby OGS's Business Services Center (\"BSC\") on behalf of its customer\nagencies to align with the OGS limit.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nAmend State Finance Law §§ 163 and 112 to increase the discretionary\npurchasing limit for transactions processed by OGS's BSC on behalf of\nits customer agencies.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAn increase in the discretionary purchase limit for transactions by the\nBSC on behalf of its customer agencies would expedite the procurement\nprocess for low-value, low-risk contracts and purchases for many State\nagencies. OGS has the processes and controls in place to manage and\noversee these purchases, just as it currently does with its own\npurchases, so extending the $85,000 limit to the BSC customer agencies\nwould pose no greater risk.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThere will be no fiscal support required to implement this bill.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
